<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  A TAKE LANE must show its take AT THE POSITION IT PLAYS, and must paint its
  MATERIAL rather than its window.

  Two defects in SMVActualView::drawTakeLane, both fixed together here:

  A (correctness) - it built a bare view context and handed the TAKE OBJECT's
    renderer the outer link, so on the `SLink -> SCut -> STakeStack` shape
    (a shared / placed take column: the stack carries nRefs > 1 and each
    placement is its own SCut window into it) every window parameter of the
    WRAPPING cut - slip, stretch, loop tiling - was dropped. The take was laid
    out 1:1 from the clip's left edge while the audio played it through the
    wrapper's window. Measured on a real project: one whole bar out. A take
    lane is the COMPING surface, so a waveform that does not line up with what
    plays is not cosmetic.

  B (consistency) - it filled the take's whole window with one opaque
    fillRect, while the composite lane has painted per-column-by-material
    since proposal 41 M5/D10. The same grey meant "material" on one lane and
    "window" on the lane directly below it.

  WHY THIS NEEDS ITS OWN AUDIO FIXTURE. The thing under test is WHICH PART of
  a take is drawn, so the material needs landmarks - and no other committed
  fixture has any. test_sawtooth / test_autosaw / test_clipsaw / test_position
  / test_stereo / test_channels4 are all continuous (longest exact-zero run:
  one sample), so every column of every window carries material and a wrong
  window is invisible to a position measurement. Windowing PAST the source end
  does not manufacture a gap either: SObject::getStraightPreview clamps its
  probe index to the last probe, so an overhang draws the last level HELD.
  Hence tests/test_gapsaw.wav (tests/tools/gen_gap_fixture.py): a 0.1 -> 0.8
  ramp with EXACT-ZERO regions at [0.6, 0.9) and [2.5, 3.0) s.

  THE ASYMMETRY IS THE GATE. tests/takestack_wrap_slipped.qxp places a 2 s
  wrapper window 2 s into the 4 s take (srcStart='96000' - and it must be
  srcStart, because SCut's loader prefers it and only falls back to
  startOffset when srcStart is ABSENT, so slipping via startOffset alone
  would leave the fixture unslipped and this case would pass on the broken
  binary):

      correct : shows source [2.0, 4.0) -> ONE gap at 25%..50% of the clip
      broken  : shows source [0.0, 2.0) -> ONE gap at 30%..45% of the clip

  Both windows contain exactly one gap, so the case cannot pass by getting
  gap PRESENCE right - it has to get the POSITION right. A periodic silence
  pattern would have produced identical relative positions in both windows and
  gated nothing.

  WHY A PIXEL VERB. Everything a script can reach through collectEnvelope sits
  BELOW the paint, which is exactly how proposal 39 M2 and proposal 41 M5 each
  shipped a green gate over a broken paint. assert-take-lane grabs the real
  arranger canvas and classifies the named take row column by column, on
  drawTakeLane's own BODY colour - never on waveform pixels, because a SILENT
  column still paints one waveform pixel at the midline (drawObjectWaveform
  maps min==max==0 to drawLine(x,y,x,y)).

  waveMeanPct is the one assertion here that rides the DRAW terminal; the gap
  metrics come from the body fill, i.e. the COLLECT terminal. Both are needed,
  and neither may be dropped: without waveMeanPct a draw-side regression is
  invisible to this case.

  Take 0 is ACTIVE (yellow frame, undrawn dim) and take 1 is INACTIVE (dimmed
  51% by a black alpha-130 fill). Both takes carry the same window, so both
  rows must report the SAME geometry - which also gates that the dim does not
  destroy the classification.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="take_lane_domain">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <load-project path="../takestack_wrap_slipped.qxp"/>

    <!-- Row 0 is track1's composite lane; expanding adds one row per take. -->
    <set-lane-view toggleTakesRow="0"/>

    <!-- THE GRID MUST BE OFF, AND IT HAS TO BE DISABLED **HERE** - after the
         load and after the lane expansion. Two separate reasons, both paid
         for by a failing run:

         (a) it matters far more than taste. A non-emphasised grid line is
             drawn in QColor(160,160,160) (sstdmixerview.cpp), which is
             EXACTLY the clip-body colour, and it is drawn OVER the lanes
             (`drawLine(x, SMV_TIME_RULER_HEIGHT, x, lanesBottom())`) - so a
             grid line is indistinguishable from material to any pixel
             classifier and reads as a full-height column of it. Measured with
             the grid on: spanLast 500 instead of 398 (a grid line at x=500,
             a hundred pixels past the clip's own end, extended the measured
             span) and the gap percentages came out 20/40 instead of 25/50.

         (b) THIS PROJECT LOADS WITH `gridVisible` TRUE WHATEVER THE FILE
             SAYS. The fixture was written with `"gridVisible":false` and it
             read back TRUE, while `"timelineZoomSecondWidth":200` out of the
             SAME properties JSON loaded correctly - so the dict is parsed and
             something re-asserts this one key after the load. A grid-disable
             placed before or immediately after `load-project` therefore finds
             the key already false, no-ops (SProject::setProp returns early
             when unchanged), and the key is true again by paint time. That is
             a real defect in its own right and is NOT fixed on this branch;
             it is recorded in the PR. Disabling here is what actually holds. -->
    <grid-disable/>

    <!-- TAKE 0, the active one (yellow frame, no dim).

         The span assertion is what stops the gate quietly widening: spanLast
         is derived from the outermost MATERIAL column, so anything else
         painted in the body colour (see the grid note above) would inflate it
         and drag every percentage off. 2 s at the fixture's 200 px/s is 400
         px; take 0's own last column carries the active-take frame, hence 398
         rather than 399, and the tolerance covers that either way. -->
    <assert-take-lane trackPath="0" takeRow="0"
                      grabWidth="900" grabHeight="600"
                      grabPng="take_lane_domain_take0.png"
                      spanFirst="1" spanLast="398" spanTolerance="2"
                      minGapCols="1" gapRuns="1"
                      firstGapStartPct="25" firstGapEndPct="50"
                      pctTolerance="3"
                      minWaveMeanPct="50" maxWaveMeanPct="75"/>

    <!-- TAKE 1, inactive and therefore dimmed 51 % by a black alpha-130 fill:
         same window, so the SAME geometry and the same drawn level. That the
         numbers survive the dim is itself the gate on the verb's dim
         handling - it composites the reference colours through Qt rather than
         reproducing its rounding. -->
    <assert-take-lane trackPath="0" takeRow="1"
                      grabWidth="900" grabHeight="600"
                      grabPng="take_lane_domain_take1.png"
                      spanFirst="1" spanLast="399" spanTolerance="2"
                      minGapCols="1" gapRuns="1"
                      firstGapStartPct="25" firstGapEndPct="50"
                      pctTolerance="3"
                      minWaveMeanPct="50" maxWaveMeanPct="75"/>
  </actions>

</SActionScript>
